; ======================================================================
;  hw_mod.pi4 - THIS BOARD'S HALF OF THE DRIVER-MODULE CONTRACT
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  REQUIRES, in this order, in the main file:
;      RaspberryPi4/Board/memmap.pi4      #MOD_ARENA_* / #MOD_STAGE_*
;      RaspberryPi4/Board/cache.pi4       CacheFlushRange
;      Anvil/Hal/abi.pbi                  the #SVCCAP_* vocabulary
;                                         (constants only; this file is
;                                         included before the table)
;
;  FOUR THINGS THE CORE CANNOT KNOW AND THIS FILE ANSWERS:
;
;    WHERE A MODULE MAY LIVE. A window of DRAM that belongs to nothing
;    else. The addresses and the five requirements that chose them are in
;    memmap.pi4 beside every other window; this file only hands them out.
;
;    HOW TO MAKE WRITTEN BYTES EXECUTABLE. The loader writes a module as
;    DATA and then branches into it, which on this architecture needs a
;    clean to the point of unification and an instruction-cache
;    invalidate over the code extent - unconditionally, whether or not a
;    relocation patched a word.
;
;    WHAT DEVICES ARE HERE. A module says what it is FOR, in compatible
;    ids covered by its digest; this board says what is PRESENT, with the
;    handle a driver for it needs. Matching the two is core arithmetic.
;    Nothing above this file learns an address from it.
;
;    WHICH SEAMS THE CORE ALREADY REACHES, and which this board could
;    have at all. Those are two different questions with two different
;    first things to check, and the capability model needs both.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE ARENA AND THE STAGING WINDOW.
;
;  Both sit inside #MOD_REGION_LO..#MOD_REGION_HI, which memmap.pi4
;  declares as one of this monitor's reserved regions - so `w`, `fill`
;  and `copy` already refuse them through HitsMonitor(), and a module is
;  protected from an operator's typo the same way the monitor's own code
;  is. They are two separate windows inside it because a container must
;  be whole and verified somewhere OUTSIDE the arena before any of it is
;  believed: the digest covers the header, and the relocation table and
;  the compatible ids sit after the image, so nothing in the file can be
;  trusted until all of it has been read.
;
;  ModArenaInit() CHECKS THIS AGAINST THE REST OF THE MAP rather than
;  taking it on trust - it walks HwMonRegion*() and HwPay*() and refuses
;  an arena overlapping any of them. HwModArenaRegion() below names the
;  index of the region that IS the arena, so the check skips the one
;  range that necessarily overlaps itself.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwModArenaBase()
  ProcedureReturn #MOD_ARENA_LO
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwModArenaBytes()
  ProcedureReturn #MOD_ARENA_BYTES
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwModArenaRegion()
  ProcedureReturn #MON_REGION_MODULES
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwModStageAddr()
  ProcedureReturn #MOD_STAGE_LO
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwModStageBytes()
  ProcedureReturn #MOD_STAGE_BYTES
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwModCodeSync(base, bytes) - the bytes the loader wrote are about to
;  be executed.
;
;  CacheFlushRange() is `dc cvau` + `ic ivau` per 64-byte line ending in
;  dsb sy + isb, which is the sequence the Arm ARM gives for concurrent
;  modification and execution of instructions (DDI 0487, B2.2.5, written
;  out under B2.4.4). It takes a CLOSED range, so the last byte is
;  base + bytes - 1 and not base + bytes; handing it the half-open end
;  would clean one line past the module, which is harmless here and wrong
;  everywhere else, so it is not done.
;
;  IT IS CALLED WHETHER OR NOT A RELOCATION PATCHED A WORD. The bytes
;  arrived as a data copy; that they needed no patching afterwards says
;  nothing about which cache they are sitting in.
;
;  pmfboot's payload path does NOT use this - it relies on the
;  dsb sy / ic ialluis / dsb sy / isb inside CallAddr(), which is enough
;  for an image entered once and never returned to. A module is called
;  back into later, so it needs the per-line sequence over its own
;  extent.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwModCodeSync(base.i, bytes.i)
  If base <= 0 Or bytes <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  CacheFlushRange(base, base + bytes - 1)
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE DEVICES THIS BOARD IS WILLING TO HAND TO A DRIVER
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  A device here is four facts: a compatible id a module can be built
;  against, the seam group it belongs to, the handle a driver needs, and
;  a phrase naming it for a person. Nothing else about it is published.
;
;  THE IDS ARE THE DEVICE TREE'S. `brcm,bcm2711-thermal` is the
;  compatible string Linux's own bcm2711_thermal driver binds to
;  (Datasheets/pi4/bcm2711.dtsi:68-77, the thermal node inside
;  avs-monitor@7d5d2000). Reusing it rather than inventing a private name
;  means a module written from the published driver documentation binds
;  to the block that documentation describes, and that a second board
;  carrying the same silicon can declare the same id without either side
;  being renamed.
;
;  THE TOKEN IS THE BLOCK'S BASE ADDRESS, and handing it over is what
;  removes the last hard-wired address from a converted driver. The
;  translation to it is the device tree's own and is not the familiar
;  one: bcm2711.dtsi:40-42 gives the soc node three ranges, and
;  0x7d5d2000 falls in the SECOND (0x7c000000 -> 0xfc000000), so the
;  processor address is $FD5D2000 and not $FE5D2000. Getting that wrong
;  reads a different peripheral, and the validity bits in the register
;  are what would catch it.
; ======================================================================
#HWDEV_AVS_MONITOR = $FD5D2000

Procedure.i HwDevCount()
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwDevIdAddr(i.i)
  Select i
    Case 0 : ProcedureReturn "brcm,bcm2711-thermal"
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwDevSeam(i.i)
  Select i
    Case 0 : ProcedureReturn #SVCCAP_THERMAL
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwDevToken(i.i)
  Select i
    Case 0 : ProcedureReturn #HWDEV_AVS_MONITOR
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; The board owns the prose about its own hardware and the core owns the
; layout of the line - the same split HwMonRegionSay() and I2cSayPins()
; are written under.
Procedure HwDevSay(i.i)
  Select i
    Case 0
      Print("the part's own temperature monitor")
    Default
      Print("a device this board does not have")
  EndSelect
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE CAPABILITY QUESTIONS A MODULE MAKES NECESSARY
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwSeamCore(id)     does the CORE reach this seam with no module at all
;  HwSeamPossible(id) is the hardware here, whether or not anything
;                     reaches it
;
;  THEY ARE SEPARATE BECAUSE A REFUSAL HAS TO TELL THREE STATES APART and
;  they have three different first things to check:
;
;    no hardware here at all        "this board exposes no ... to Anvil"
;    the core reaches it            no refusal; the command runs
;    here, but nothing fills it     "... and no module is loaded that
;                                   fills it. `mod` shows what is loaded."
;
;  #SVCCAP_THERMAL IS THE INTERESTING ONE AND IT ANSWERS 1 TO BOTH. The
;  core still reaches a temperature through the firmware property
;  interface with no module loaded, so the capability is 1 either way;
;  what a loaded module changes is the SOURCE - a register read the fan
;  policy can afford on every tick, instead of a mailbox round trip that
;  a busy firmware can starve. A capability model that reported that as
;  "gained a capability" would be lying in the operator's favour, which
;  is the worst direction.
;
;  #SVCCAP_PWM is declared possible and not core-reachable: the hardware
;  is on this part and the seam id is reserved, but no runtime slot block
;  exists for it yet. That is a reservation, not an implementation, and
;  saying so here is cheaper than two lanes discovering the id at once.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i HwSeamCore(id.i)
  Select id
    Case #SVCCAP_THERMAL : ProcedureReturn 1
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwSeamPossible(id.i)
  Select id
    Case #SVCCAP_THERMAL : ProcedureReturn 1
    Case #SVCCAP_PWM     : ProcedureReturn 1
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
